  "prompt_text": "This schematic illustrates a two-way interaction between sensory dorsal root ganglion (DRG) neurons and pancreatic cancer cells that promotes nerve-related disease. On the left, DRG neurons are shown undergoing neural remodelling, suggesting structural changes in response to tumour signals. On the right, a pancreatic tumour sits within pancreatic tissue. Between them, the chemokines CXCL10 and CCL21 appear to act as messengers connecting the two cell types.\n\nThe central idea is that these signals drive migration of cancer cells toward neurons, while also feeding back to alter the neurons themselves. In the tumour cells, the figure highlights AKT signalling through the receptors CXCR3 and CCR7, implying that chemokine-receptor activation supports invasive behaviour. Curved arrows in both directions emphasize reciprocal communication rather than a one-way process.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a mechanism in which pancreatic cancer and sensory neurons mutually influence each other: tumour-derived or neuron-associated chemokine cues attract cells to nerves, neurons remodel, and intracellular AKT pathway activation in cancer cells contributes to this interaction. The combined outcome is summarized at the bottom as neural invasion and pain.",
